From: Chad Monroe <chad@monroe.io>
Date: Fri, 15 May 2026 04:58:42 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: mediatek: bypass HQoS for rate-uncapped PPPQ flows on netsys v3

The MT7988 HQoS engine has a ~10 Gbps end-to-end throughput cap. With
qos_toggle=2 (the PPPQ default after 999-3025), every offloaded flow is
stamped with an IB2 QID by mtk_foe_entry_set_queue(), which on netsys v3
also sets MTK_FOE_TPORT_IDX=1 and routes the packet through the QDMA
scheduler. Two 10 G GMACs (SDG-8732v, 9000) therefore cannot reach
bidirectional line rate while traffic transits HQoS.

The vendor patch 999-3021 commit log states: "Without this patch, the
ETH/WiFi to ETH traffic may not enter the QDMA, even if IB2_PSE_QOS is
set on the MT7988." Leaving TPORT_IDX at 0 therefore keeps the flow out
of the HQoS engine.

When the egress data queue has MTK_QTX_SCH_MAX_RATE_EN_V3 clear there is
no shaping work to do, so skip mtk_foe_entry_set_queue() entirely. The
FOE entry keeps IB2.QID / PSE_QOS / TPORT_IDX at zero, the flow bypasses
HQoS, and line-rate forwarding is restored. As soon as a port negotiates
a sub-10 G speed and mtk_set_queue_speed() programs MAX_RATE_EN on its
PPPQ queue, subsequent offload binds for that egress get HQoS-stamped
again. No active FOE flush is required: the only path that toggles
MAX_RATE_EN runs from the NETDEV_CHANGE notifier, which implies a
momentary link drop that already invalidates any in-flight flows.

Gate restricted to netsys v3, where TPORT_IDX exists. Behaviour on
netsys v1/v2 is unchanged.

Signed-off-by: Chad Monroe <chad@monroe.io>
---
 drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_eth_soc.c     |   17 ++++++++++++++
 drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_eth_soc.h     |    1 
 drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_ppe_offload.c |   10 +++++++-
 3 files changed, 27 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_eth_soc.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_eth_soc.c
@@ -1022,6 +1022,31 @@ void mtk_set_queue_sched(struct mtk_eth
 	mtk_w32(eth, val, soc->reg_map->qdma.qtx_sch + ofs);
 }
 
+bool mtk_queue_has_rate_cap(struct mtk_eth *eth, unsigned int idx)
+{
+	const struct mtk_soc_data *soc = eth->soc;
+	u32 ofs, val, mask;
+
+	if (!MTK_HAS_CAPS(soc->caps, MTK_QDMA))
+		return false;
+
+	mtk_w32(eth, (idx / MTK_QTX_PER_PAGE), soc->reg_map->qdma.page);
+	ofs = MTK_QTX_OFFSET * (idx % MTK_QTX_PER_PAGE);
+	val = mtk_r32(eth, soc->reg_map->qdma.qtx_sch + ofs);
+
+	/* Match the register-layout switch in mtk_set_queue_speed(): netsys
+	 * v3 silicon other than MT7988 uses the wider _V3 fields; MT7988
+	 * (despite being v3) keeps the V1/V2 layout, so MAX_RATE_EN lives
+	 * at bit 11 there.
+	 */
+	if (mtk_is_netsys_v3_or_greater(eth) && soc->caps != MT7988_CAPS)
+		mask = MTK_QTX_SCH_MAX_RATE_EN_V3;
+	else
+		mask = MTK_QTX_SCH_MAX_RATE_EN;
+
+	return !!(val & mask);
+}
+
 static void mtk_gdm_mac_setup_eee(struct mtk_mac *mac, bool enable)
 {
 	struct mtk_eth *eth = mac->hw;
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_eth_soc.h
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_eth_soc.h
@@ -1863,5 +1863,6 @@ void mtk_eth_set_dma_device(struct mtk_e
 void mtk_eth_set_dsa_pppq_offset(struct mtk_eth *eth);
 void mtk_eth_set_dsa_user_idx(struct mtk_eth *eth, struct net_device *dev);
 void mtk_set_queue_sched(struct mtk_eth *eth, unsigned int idx, u32 sch_id);
+bool mtk_queue_has_rate_cap(struct mtk_eth *eth, unsigned int idx);
 
 #endif /* MTK_ETH_H */
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_ppe_offload.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_ppe_offload.c
@@ -338,7 +338,15 @@ mtk_flow_set_output_device(struct mtk_et
 		ct_mark = ct->mark;
 	}
 
-	if (eth->qos_toggle == 2 && mtk_ppe_check_pppq_path(mac, idev, dsa_port)) {
+	if (eth->qos_toggle == 2 && mtk_ppe_check_pppq_path(mac, idev, dsa_port) &&
+	    (!mtk_is_netsys_v3_or_greater(eth) ||
+	     mtk_queue_has_rate_cap(eth, queue))) {
+		/* On netsys v3, skipping mtk_foe_entry_set_queue() leaves
+		 * IB2.PSE_QOS and the v3 TPORT_IDX field at zero so the flow
+		 * bypasses the HQoS engine (subject to its ~10 Gbps cap).
+		 * Only stamp the PPPQ qid when the egress data queue has a
+		 * max-rate cap programmed and therefore actually needs shaping.
+		 */
 		if (ct && nf_ct_protonum(ct) == IPPROTO_TCP) {
 			/* Dispatch IPv4/IPv6 TCP ACKs (<= 64 bytes) into the
 			 * high-priority half of the PPPQ range.
